{"name":"Soul Quenching","hex":"#7e989d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"soul","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The spirit or essence of a person usually thought to consist of one's thoughts and personality, often believed to live on after the person's death.","The spirit or essence of anything."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Characteristic of or pertaining to African American culture."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soul"},{"term":"quenching","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The extinction of any of several physical properties.","The rapid cooling of a hot metal object, by placing it in a liquid, in order to harden it."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of quench"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/quenching"}],"entries":[{"title":"Soul","blocks":[{"text":"In religion and philosophy, the soul is the immaterial aspect or essence of a living being. It is typically believed to be immortal and to exist apart from the material world. Anthropologists and psychologists have found that most humans believe in the existence of a soul or spirit, and that they have cross-culturally distinguished between the soul and the material body."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Schiavonetti_Soul_leaving_body_1808.jpg/330px-Schiavonetti_Soul_leaving_body_1808.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soul","wikidata":"Q9165"},{"title":"Quenching","blocks":[{"text":"In materials science, quenching is the rapid cooling of a workpiece in water, gas, oil, polymer, air, or other fluids to obtain certain material properties. A type of heat treating, quenching prevents undesired low-temperature processes, such as phase transformations, from occurring. It does this by reducing the window of time during which these undesired reactions are both thermodynamically favorable and kinetically accessible; for instance, quenching can reduce the crystal grain size of both metallic and plastic materials, increasing their hardness."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/ArthurSiegelcoke1.jpg/330px-ArthurSiegelcoke1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quenching","wikidata":"Q871335"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/The_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg/500px-The_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/The_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg/500px-The_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bhavachakra describing the cycle of saṃsāra; the wheel of life caused by craving to a self, thus giving raise to rebirths in one of the six realms of existence.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/SoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg/500px-SoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/SoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg/500px-SoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of a soul being carried to heaven by two angels by William-Adolphe Bouguereau","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Miraj_by_Sultan_Muhammad.jpg/500px-Miraj_by_Sultan_Muhammad.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Miraj_by_Sultan_Muhammad.jpg/128